---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import WorkedTable from '../../components/insights/WorkedTable.astro';
import PractitionerNote from '../../components/insights/PractitionerNote.astro';
import { practitionerB2 } from '../../data/practitioner-b2';
import examples from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14-operations.json';
const e = practitionerB2[0];
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type="Analysis" readTime="5 min" standfirst="A 50% batch discount is a price condition. Your workflow also needs a completion window, a way to identify missing results and enough time to recover them.">
  <p>An overnight tagging job looks like an obvious candidate for batch processing. Nobody is watching a chat window, the requests are independent and the results can arrive together. Before moving it, put the actual deadline in the tracker. “Ready for tomorrow” is too vague if the next system starts at six in the morning.</p>
  <p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>Message Batches guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, offers a 50% discount and says batches expire after 24 hours. A job submitted at ten in the evening therefore needs more planning than the word “overnight” suggests. A completion window extending into the following evening does not promise results by breakfast.</p>
  <h2>Price the recovery path</h2>
  <p>Consider 1,000 independent jobs. Assume an ordinary request costs $0.02 and the batch version costs $0.01. These are invented rates. Also assume that an expired request is unbilled and can be completed with one ordinary request. Anthropic documents unbilled expiries; the number of expiries and successful recoveries below are assumptions.</p>
  <WorkedTable headers={['Assumed expiries', 'Batch completions', 'Bill after fallback', 'Saving against $20 ordinary run']} rows={examples.batch} caption="Worked scenarios, not completion forecasts. Each expiry gets one successful $0.02 fallback; completed batch requests cost $0.01. Other costs excluded." />
  <p>With 100 expiries the final bill is $11, a 45% saving against the $20 ordinary run. The arithmetic still looks good. The deadline may not: if you wait for expiry before starting recovery, those requests begin after the batch's completion window. You need to decide whether that is acceptable before choosing the route.</p>
  <h2>One completed batch can contain several outcomes</h2>
  <p>The provider distinguishes succeeded, errored, cancelled and expired requests. Give each outcome its own count. The batch being finished means processing has ended; it does not mean that each document has a usable answer.</p>
  <p>Keep a manifest of the jobs you submitted and match results back to it. The guide uses a unique custom_id and says results are not ordered. Join by that identifier. Do not attach the first answer to the first document simply because the files have the same number of lines.</p>
  <p>Our suggested reconciliation is simple: each submitted identifier must have one recorded final disposition. A successful response then goes through the task's usual acceptance check. A format failure is different from a request that never ran, and the recovery policy should preserve that distinction.</p>
  <h2>Reserve time for a second attempt</h2>
  <p>Write the deadline backwards. Allow time to download and reconcile results, validate them and recover missing work. If the provider's processing window leaves no room for those steps, submit earlier or use the ordinary route for the deadline-sensitive part.</p>
  <p>If you start a fallback while the batch is still processing, plan for both attempts to finish. Use the job identifier to prevent duplicate downstream writes and account for both bills where applicable. The table assumes recovery only after unbilled expiry. It would understate a strategy that pays for two completed copies.</p>
  <p>Try a representative small batch before moving the back catalogue. Check the request shape and inspect the returned usage and outcome fields. Record elapsed time for that run as an observation, without promoting it into a service guarantee.</p>
  <h2>Keep cache savings out of the first estimate</h2>
  <p>The batch guide says caching can combine with batch pricing, but cache hits are best effort. Start your budget with the batch rate alone. Add a cache saving after you have measured reads and writes for the request pattern. Otherwise two attractive discounts can produce a forecast that neither feature promises.</p>
  <p>A useful tracker row names the batch route, submitted count, accepted count, final spend, submission time and deadline. Add outcome counts and the recovery rule. You can then decide whether the discount bought useful work in time, rather than merely cheaper requests.</p>
  <PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
